  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA weak and fairly small upper-level ridge will be sitting over SD \nthis afternoon through the weekend. This ridge, as well as winds \nfrom the east and southeast aloft and at the surface will help to \nmove warm and moist air in over SD. This will create a saturated \natmospheric environment over central and northeastern SD through the \nremainder of today and into Friday. PWAT values across central and \nnortheastern SD will be around 1.75 inches, which is above the 90th \npercentile and the maximum value for this time of the year. This \nshows how much moisture there is over central and northeastern SD. A \nlow pressure system is moving out of Colorado today and will move \nover southern SD tonight, into this moist environment. Precipitation \nalong the northern and western side of the low will move into \ncentral SD this evening and then over northeastern SD and west \ncentral MN tonight. There is a little bit of instability over \nsouthern SD moving in with this system this evening and overnight. \nThis could help a few storms to develop and dump a lot of rain in \nportions of south central SD and a heavier band of precipitation to \ndevelop over east central SD late tonight into Friday morning.\n\nModels have been fairly consistent with the location of a heavy band \nof precipitation, with the heaviest rainfall occurring over south \ncentral SD, in a line from around Vivian to Miller and Redfield, \nlate this evening and overnight. Additionally, this band of heavy \nrainfall will move over east central SD between Watertown and Sioux \nFalls tonight into Friday morning. There is currently a 40-60% \nchance of areas along this line to receive 2 inches of rainfall or \nmore. There is a bit more uncertainty about the northern edge of \nthis line of precipitation and how far north the chances for 1 inch \nor more of rainfall will be. Models show a 40-60% chance for an inch \nor more of rain in a line from Eagle Butte to Leola and in areas to \nthe southeast until it runs into the line of heaviest precipitation \nmentions before, as this line has 70-90% chance. The saturated \natmosphere will help to get these rainfall totals but the strength \nand timing of the band of precipitation will also contribute. With \nthe instability over south central SD and the ability for convection \nto develop, the rain will likely be heavier for a shorter period of \ntime over this area. East central SD on the other hand will have the \nprecipitation around for a much longer time, leading to higher \nrainfall amounts. There is a slight risk for excessive rainfall from \nthis system over most of central and northeastern SD tonight into \nFriday. While the ground should be able to take in a lot of the \nwater, depending on how fast it comes down and in areas that get an \nabundant amount of rain, there could be some impacts in low lying \nand poor drainage areas as well as some localized areas of flooding. \nAdditionally, there will be minor impacts to rivers and streams from \nthe potential runoff of the rainfall. \n\nAfter Friday, there will be a couple of shortwaves that move over \ncentral and northeastern SD this weekend. This will lead to some more \nscattered rain showers moving through occasionally during the \nweekend. There will be some cooler temperatures moving in with the \nlow pressure system starting Friday into the weekend. Temperatures \nat the surface will be around to slightly below normal, with highs \ngetting into the low 60s to low 70s.",
